The asPath() method of org.apache.commons.jxpath.ri.model.dom.NamespacePointer recursively calls parent.asPath() and appends the namespace prefix with "namespace::".

From the bug report, the issue is an endless loop when calling asPath() on a node with a "resetting the default namespace" scenario like:

```
<b:foo xmlns:b="bla" xmlns="test111">
  <b:bar>a</b:bar>
  <test xmlns=""></test>
</b:foo>
```

Calling asPath() on the 'test' node causes this loop.

The asPath method:

```java
public String asPath() {
    StringBuffer buffer = new StringBuffer();
    if (parent != null) {
        buffer.append(parent.asPath());
        if (buffer.length() == 0 || buffer.charAt(buffer.length() - 1) != '/') {
            buffer.append('/');
        }
    }
    buffer.append("namespace::");
    buffer.append(prefix);
    return buffer.toString();
}
```

If parent.asPath() itself calls down back to this asPath() creating a circular reference, this will cause an endless recursion.

The missing piece to confirm is how the parent pointer is assigned or retrieved for NamespacePointer. The bug likely arises when the parent pointer chain leads back to this pointer or to another pointer that leads back to this pointer due to namespace resetting.

To verify, I try to locate how parent is defined in NamespacePointer and how it may cause cycles.

Since "parent" couldn't be fetched directly from NamespacePointer (seems to be a field), I'll look at the NamespacePointer class's fields and constructor to understand parent pointer assignment.

